  description="Contact the WrongfulTerminationCalc editorial team with questions, corrections, or feedback. We do not provide legal advice."
>

  <h1>Contact</h1>

  <p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We're a small team, so email is the only channel we monitor &mdash; there's no live chat or phone line, and that's by design: wrongful termination questions deserve a considered written answer, not a rushed one.</p>

  <h2>What we respond to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrections to legal citations or damages figures.</strong> Federal damages caps, state statutes of limitations, and EEOC filing deadlines get updated periodically. If a page cites a figure, deadline, or statute that's out of date, tell us which page and what the correct information is; we verify against the primary source before making any change.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors or unclear language in guides.</strong> If a sentence in a guide is ambiguous, contradicts itself, or is just hard to follow, we want to know &mdash; plain language is one of our core editorial standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions for new content.</strong> If you searched for a wrongful termination question and couldn't find it on the Site &mdash; a specific state's protections, a claim type the calculator doesn't handle &mdash; let us know what you were trying to figure out.</li>
    <li><strong>Press and business inquiries.</strong> Journalists covering employment law or workplace policy, and businesses interested in advertising, can reach the same address.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>We read every message. Corrections and editorial questions typically get a reply within three to five business days. If a correction is verified, we update the affected page and note the date of the material change. Our full editorial standards and review process are on the <a href="/editorial/">editorial team page</a>. We don't always reply to content suggestions individually, but we do track them for future guides.</p>

  <h2>What we don't respond to</h2>
  <p>We can't evaluate your specific termination, tell you whether you have a viable claim, estimate what your case is actually worth beyond the calculator's general ranges, or review your personnel file or severance offer &mdash; that requires an attorney who has seen the specific facts, documents, and timeline. We also don't recommend specific attorneys or law firms, and we don't accept payment to feature or promote anyone on the Site. If you email us asking for case-specific advice, we'll point you back to this policy and to the guidance below.</p>

  <h2>Before you email</h2>
  <p>Many questions we get are already answered on the Site: check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> for common questions about at-will employment, filing deadlines, and documentation, or the <a href="/guides/">guides</a> &mdash; including <a href="/guides/how-wrongful-termination-damages-are-calculated/">how wrongful termination damages are calculated</a>, <a href="/guides/signs-your-termination-may-have-been-wrongful/">signs your termination may have been wrongful</a>, <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-wrongful-termination/">what to do after wrongful termination</a>, and <a href="/guides/wrongful-termination-vs-at-will-employment/">wrongful termination vs. at-will employment</a>. If your question is about how the calculator arrived at a number, the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> walks through the back pay, front pay, and damages-cap formulas in detail.</p>

  <h2>If you're deciding whether to talk to an attorney</h2>
  <p>We don't make referrals, but one distinction matters more than any other before you search: at-will employment means an employer can generally fire you for a bad reason, an unfair reason, or no reason at all, and that alone usually isn't illegal. A termination only becomes wrongful when a specific legal theory applies &mdash; breach of a written or implied employment contract, discrimination based on a protected characteristic, retaliation for a protected activity like reporting harassment or filing a workers' comp claim, or a firing that violates established public policy. Two people can be fired in outwardly similar circumstances and only one of them has a claim, because the difference lives in facts like timing, comparators, and documentation, not in how unfair the firing felt. That's exactly the kind of judgment call a calculator can't make and a consultation can: an employment attorney's first job in an initial consultation is usually to figure out whether one of these theories actually fits your facts, not to tell you the termination was wrong in some general sense. Most employment attorneys offer a free initial consultation and work on contingency for these cases, so identifying whether a viable theory exists rarely costs anything upfront. Filing deadlines can be short &mdash; some EEOC charges must be filed within 180 to 300 days of the termination &mdash; so this is not a decision worth sitting on. If you'd rather start from a neutral referral source, your state bar association's lawyer referral service or the EEOC at <a href="https://www.eeoc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eeoc.gov</a> can point you in the right direction.</p>

